## Findings
1. blocking — scripts/exact-digits.mjs:174 The recursion depth is not advanced when following variable writes, so a cyclic chain such as `let a = b; let b = a; const m = a.exec(s); Number(m[1])` makes `npm run lint` fail with `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` instead of leaving the unrecognised form unjudged, contrary to brief:80. [severity: S1] [class: mechanism×cycle-guard-not-propagated] — check: packages/cli/src/safe-helpers.test.ts
2. blocking — scripts/exact-digits.mjs:40 `captureGroup` includes a named group’s `?<name>` prefix in the text passed to `maxDigits`, so `Number(m[1])` for `/(?<group1234567890123456>a)/` is falsely reported as a 16-digit capture even though the capture contains no digits; named groups are inside the declared grammar at brief:52. [severity: S1] [class: mechanism×regex-metadata-counted-as-digits] — check: packages/cli/src/safe-helpers.test.ts
3. blocking — scripts/exact-digits.mjs:281 The rule identifies numeric readers solely by identifier spelling, so a locally shadowed `Number` function that returns a string is treated as the built-in double conversion and fails lint; source text is the declared input domain and unrecognised forms must remain unjudged at brief:80. [severity: S1] [class: mechanism×shadowed-global-treated-as-builtin] — check: packages/cli/src/safe-helpers.test.ts
4. advisory — scripts/exact-digits.mjs:61 A mixed character class such as `[0-9A-F]+` can match an arbitrarily long all-digit value, but `digitClass` excludes it and `Number(m[1])` is unreported. This form is outside the brief’s listed digit-only-class grammar; a lint case in the behaviour-1 row would pin it if the architect rules it in. [severity: S3] [class: mechanism×unpinned-by-test] — check: packages/cli/src/safe-helpers.test.ts

